import RequestProject.Wikidata.Core

/-!
# Wikidata value types: quantities, times and globe coordinates

The engine in `RequestProject.Wikidata.Core` is item-valued: a statement points an
item at an item.  That is the fragment the class order lives in, but it is not the
whole data model.  Wikidata also carries *literal* value types, and each of them
comes with structure that a naive reading throws away:

* a **quantity** is an amount *with a unit* and *with bounds* — an interval, not a
  number, so the right comparison is interval overlap in a shared unit;
* a **time** is a timestamp *with a precision* and *with a calendar model* — the
  fields below the stated precision carry no information, so equality must be read
  at a precision;
* a **globe coordinate** is a latitude/longitude pair *with a precision* and *with
  a globe* — the same numbers on Earth and on Mars are different places.

Each type gets an executable comparison with an exactness theorem, and each gets
the negative result that says which naive reading is wrong: a bare amount is not a
quantity (`amount_equality_is_not_quantity_agreement`), interval overlap is not
transitive (`overlaps_not_transitive`), a coarse time is not a claim about the
finer fields (`year_precision_does_not_determine_the_day`), a calendar model is
part of the value (`numeric_equality_is_not_time_agreement`), and coordinate
matching is neither symmetric in general nor transitive.
-/

